# References Section

## Purpose

Defines the References section of the MAM specification. The section lists external resources such as documentation, standards, and related projects so that a module can carry its own knowledge context.

## Inputs

| Name | Type | Required | Description |
|------|------|----------|-------------|
| content | string | Yes | Full MAM module text that contains the section |
| refs | list | No | Reference declarations to validate directly |

## Outputs

| Name | Type | Description |
|------|------|-------------|
| refs | list | Extracted reference declarations |
| valid | bool | Whether the references pass validation |
| errors | list | Validation errors found in the references |

## Capabilities

### validate

Checks URLs, HTTPS usage, descriptive text, duplicates, and secrets.

### extract

Pulls the reference declarations out of the section body.

### document

Renders the references into a documentation fragment.

## Rules

- All URLs must be valid and accessible.
- Link text should describe the destination.
- Links should not be broken.
- Resources should be relevant to the module.
- Never include URLs with secrets or tokens.
- Prefer HTTPS when available.
- Group related references when helpful.

## Workflow

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A[Content] --> B[Extract]
    B --> C[Validate]
    C --> D[Document]
    D --> E[Output]
```

## Python

```python
import re

def validate_url(url: str) -> bool:
    return bool(re.match(r"^https?://[^\s]+$", url))

def check_https(url: str) -> bool:
    return url.startswith("https://")

def check_secrets(url: str) -> bool:
    return "secret" not in url.lower() and "token" not in url.lower() and "key=" not in url.lower()

def check_descriptive(text: str) -> bool:
    return len(text.strip()) >= 3

def check_duplicates(refs: list) -> list:
    seen = set()
    duplicates = []
    for ref in refs:
        if ref in seen:
            duplicates.append(ref)
        seen.add(ref)
    return duplicates

def extract_references(content: str) -> list:
    refs = []
    in_section = False
    for line in content.split("\n"):
        if line.strip().startswith("## References"):
            in_section = True
            continue
        if in_section and line.startswith("##"):
            break
        if in_section and line.strip().startswith("- ["):
            refs.append(line.strip()[2:])
    return refs

def validate_references(refs: list) -> list:
    errors = []
    for ref in refs:
        match = re.match(r"\[([^\]]+)\]\((https?://[^)]+)\)", ref)
        if not match:
            errors.append("Invalid reference format: " + ref)
            continue
        url = match.group(2)
        if not validate_url(url):
            errors.append("Invalid URL: " + url)
        if not check_https(url):
            errors.append("URL is not HTTPS: " + url)
        if not check_secrets(url):
            errors.append("URL may contain a secret: " + url)
        if not check_descriptive(match.group(1)):
            errors.append("Reference text is not descriptive: " + ref)
    return errors

def document_references(refs: list) -> str:
    docs = "## External References\n\n"
    for ref in refs:
        docs += "- " + ref + "\n"
    return docs
```

## Tests

### Input

```yaml
content: |
  ## References

  - [Python Documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/)
  - [MAM Specification](https://example.com/spec)
```

### Expected

```yaml
valid: true
errors: []
```

```python
def test_references():
    refs = ["[Python Docs](https://docs.python.org/3/)"]
    assert validate_url("https://docs.python.org/3/")
    assert check_https("https://docs.python.org/3/")
    assert check_secrets("https://example.com/page")
    assert check_descriptive("Python Docs")
    assert validate_references(refs) == []
    assert check_duplicates(refs) == []
    assert "External References" in document_references(refs)
```

## Examples

### Official Documentation

```markdown
- [Python Documentation](https://docs.python.org/3/)  Official Python docs
- [FastAPI Documentation](https://fastapi.tiangolo.com/)  FastAPI framework
```

### Standards

```markdown
- [RFC 7519](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc7519)  JSON Web Token specification
- [RFC 6749](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc6749)  OAuth 2.0 framework
```

